# Selecto Set Operations Workbook

## Setup 1) Install Dependencies

Run this cell first. It installs the local `selecto_livebooks` project and its
current Selecto dependencies for this Livebook session.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install!()
```

## Overview

Set operations combine two compatible query shapes.

1. `union/3` for A ∪ B
2. `intersect/3` for A ∩ B
3. `except/3` for A − B
4. `all: true` variants to keep duplicates

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  a[Query A]
  b[Query B]
  compat[Same selected columns]
  setop[UNION / INTERSECT / EXCEPT]
  out[Combined result set]

  a --> compat
  b --> compat
  compat --> setop --> out
```

```mermaid
graph TD
  cohortA[Employees with manager]
  cohortB[Employees with direct reports]
  union[UNION: A or B]
  inter[INTERSECT: A and B]
  exceptA[EXCEPT: A not B]

  cohortA --> union
  cohortB --> union
  cohortA --> inter
  cohortB --> inter
  cohortA --> exceptA
```

Before running, initialize the sample database:

```bash
mix setup
```

## Setup 2) Connect to Repo and Build Employee Domain

This cell defines a local Repo and employee domain used to build compatible cohorts.

```elixir
defmodule SetOpsLab.Repo do
  use Ecto.Repo,
    otp_app: :set_ops_lab_livebook,
    adapter: Ecto.Adapters.Postgres
end

defmodule SetOpsLab.EmployeeDomain do
  @moduledoc false

  def domain do
    %{
      name: "Employees",
      source: %{
        source_table: "employees",
        primary_key: :id,
        fields: [:id, :first_name, :last_name, :title, :department, :manager_id, :active],
        columns: %{
          id: %{type: :integer},
          first_name: %{type: :string},
          last_name: %{type: :string},
          title: %{type: :string},
          department: %{type: :string},
          manager_id: %{type: :integer},
          active: %{type: :boolean}
        },
        associations: %{}
      },
      schemas: %{},
      joins: %{},
      default_selected: ["id", "first_name", "last_name", "title"],
      default_order_by: [{"id", :asc}]
    }
  end
end

repo_config = [
  database: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB", "selecto_livebooks_dev"),
  username: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_USER", "postgres"),
  password: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_PASS", "postgres"),
  hostname: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_HOST", "localhost"),
  port: String.to_integer(System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_PORT", "5432")),
  pool_size: 5
]

case Process.whereis(SetOpsLab.Repo) do
  nil -> {:ok, _pid} = SetOpsLab.Repo.start_link(repo_config)
  _pid -> :ok
end

config = %{
  repo: SetOpsLab.Repo,
  employee_domain: SetOpsLab.EmployeeDomain.domain()
}

{:ok, result} = Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, "select count(*) from employees", [])
[[employee_count]] = result.rows
IO.puts("Connected. Employees in dataset: #{employee_count}")

config
```

## Setup 3) Define Set-Op Helpers

This helper cell prints SQL/params and executes a query with a compact preview.

```elixir
defmodule SetOpsLab.Helpers do
  @moduledoc false

  def run(label, meaning, query, preview_count \\ 10) do
    IO.puts("Meaning: #{meaning}")
    Selecto.Livebook.run(label, query, preview_count: preview_count)
  end
end
```

## Setup 4) Current Expression DSL Imports

This workbook keeps the set-operation calls explicit, but now uses the current
expression DSL for the cohort-building queries that feed those set operations.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

:ok
```

## 1) Build Compatible Cohorts

Both cohorts select the same columns so they can participate in set operations.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

employees_with_manager =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([id, first_name, last_name, title]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(manager_id)))

employees_with_direct_reports =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.join(:direct_report,
    source: "employees",
    type: :inner,
    owner_key: :id,
    related_key: :manager_id,
    fields: %{
      id: %{type: :integer}
    }
  )
  |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(direct_report.id)))
  |> Selecto.select(select([id, first_name, last_name, title]))
  |> Selecto.group_by(group_by([id, first_name, last_name, title]))

:ok
```

## 2) UNION and UNION ALL

`UNION` de-duplicates; `UNION ALL` keeps duplicates.

```elixir
union_query =
  Selecto.union(employees_with_manager, employees_with_direct_reports)

union_all_query =
  Selecto.union(employees_with_manager, employees_with_direct_reports, all: true)

SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
  "UNION",
  "Employees who either report to someone or manage at least one direct report.",
  union_query
)

SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
  "UNION ALL",
  "Same cohorts but keep duplicates (middle managers may appear twice).",
  union_all_query
)
```

## 3) INTERSECT

`INTERSECT` returns employees in both cohorts.

```elixir
intersect_query =
  Selecto.intersect(employees_with_manager, employees_with_direct_reports)

SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
  "INTERSECT",
  "Middle managers: employees who report upward and also manage others.",
  intersect_query
)
```

## 4) EXCEPT (Directional)

Direction matters: `A EXCEPT B` is different from `B EXCEPT A`.

```elixir
except_a_minus_b =
  Selecto.except(employees_with_manager, employees_with_direct_reports)

except_b_minus_a =
  Selecto.except(employees_with_direct_reports, employees_with_manager)

SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
  "EXCEPT (A - B)",
  "Employees with managers who do not manage anyone (individual contributors).",
  except_a_minus_b
)

SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
  "EXCEPT (B - A)",
  "Employees who manage others but do not report upward (top-level leadership).",
  except_b_minus_a
)
```

## 5) Summary Runner

Run all five comparisons in sequence to inspect SQL and row counts side by side.

```elixir
set_queries = [
  {
    "UNION",
    "A ∪ B",
    Selecto.union(employees_with_manager, employees_with_direct_reports)
  },
  {
    "UNION ALL",
    "A ∪ B (duplicates kept)",
    Selecto.union(employees_with_manager, employees_with_direct_reports, all: true)
  },
  {
    "INTERSECT",
    "A ∩ B",
    Selecto.intersect(employees_with_manager, employees_with_direct_reports)
  },
  {
    "EXCEPT (A - B)",
    "A minus B",
    Selecto.except(employees_with_manager, employees_with_direct_reports)
  },
  {
    "EXCEPT (B - A)",
    "B minus A",
    Selecto.except(employees_with_direct_reports, employees_with_manager)
  }
]

Enum.each(set_queries, fn {label, meaning, query} ->
  SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(label, meaning, query, 5)
end)

:ok
```

## 6) Cross-Domain Set-Op with `column_mapping`

Use `column_mapping` to union compatible value types across domains with different field names.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

customer_domain_for_set_ops = %{
  name: "Customers",
  source: %{
    source_table: "customers",
    primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :name, :email, :country, :active],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer},
      name: %{type: :string},
      email: %{type: :string},
      country: %{type: :string},
      active: %{type: :boolean}
    },
    associations: %{}
  },
  schemas: %{},
  joins: %{}
}

employees_identity_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([id, first_name, last_name, department]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(id)))
  |> Selecto.limit(25)

customers_identity_query =
  Selecto.configure(customer_domain_for_set_ops, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([id, name, email, country]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(id)))
  |> Selecto.limit(25)

cross_domain_union_query =
  Selecto.union(
    employees_identity_query,
    customers_identity_query,
    all: true,
    column_mapping: [
      {"first_name", "name"},
      {"last_name", "email"},
      {"department", "country"}
    ]
  )

SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
  "Cross-domain UNION ALL with column_mapping",
  "Employee identity rows and customer identity rows combined via mapped columns.",
  cross_domain_union_query,
  10
)
```

## 7) Deduplicating vs Non-Deduplicating Timing Snapshot

Compare execution timing for `UNION` and `UNION ALL` over repeated runs.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

build_base_cohorts = fn ->
  employees_with_manager_local =
    Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
    |> Selecto.select(select([id, first_name, last_name, title]))
    |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(manager_id)))

  employees_with_direct_reports_local =
    Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
    |> Selecto.join(:direct_report,
      source: "employees",
      type: :inner,
      owner_key: :id,
      related_key: :manager_id,
      fields: %{id: %{type: :integer}}
    )
    |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(direct_report.id)))
    |> Selecto.select(select([id, first_name, last_name, title]))
    |> Selecto.group_by(group_by([id, first_name, last_name, title]))

  {
    Selecto.union(employees_with_manager_local, employees_with_direct_reports_local),
    Selecto.union(employees_with_manager_local, employees_with_direct_reports_local, all: true)
  }
end

{bench_union_query, bench_union_all_query} = build_base_cohorts.()

benchmark = fn label, query, iterations ->
  samples =
    Enum.map(1..iterations, fn _ ->
      {microseconds, result} = :timer.tc(fn -> Selecto.execute(query) end)

      row_count =
        case result do
          {:ok, {rows, _, _}} -> length(rows)
          _ -> :error
        end

      {microseconds / 1000.0, row_count}
    end)

  ms_samples = Enum.map(samples, fn {ms, _} -> ms end)
  avg_ms = Enum.sum(ms_samples) / max(length(ms_samples), 1)

  IO.puts("\n#{label}")
  IO.puts("avg ms over #{iterations} runs: #{Float.round(avg_ms, 3)}")
  IO.inspect(Enum.take(samples, 3), label: "sample runs (ms, row_count)")
end

benchmark.("UNION (deduplicating)", bench_union_query, 6)
benchmark.("UNION ALL (non-deduplicating)", bench_union_all_query, 6)
```

## 8) Set Operations with CTE-Staged Cohorts

Build cohorts through CTEs first, then combine them with set operations.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

staged_has_manager_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.with_cte("has_manager_ids", fn ->
    Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
    |> Selecto.select(select([id]))
    |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(manager_id)))
  end,
  columns: ["id"],
  join: [type: :inner, owner_key: :id, related_key: :id, fields: :infer]
  )
  |> Selecto.select(select([id, first_name, last_name, title]))

staged_is_manager_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.with_cte("manager_ids", fn ->
    Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
    |> Selecto.select(select([manager_id]))
    |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(manager_id)))
    |> Selecto.group_by(group_by([manager_id]))
  end,
  columns: ["id"],
  join: [type: :inner, owner_key: :id, related_key: :id, fields: :infer]
  )
  |> Selecto.select(select([id, first_name, last_name, title]))

staged_intersection_query =
  Selecto.intersect(staged_has_manager_query, staged_is_manager_query)

SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
  "CTE-staged INTERSECT",
  "Employees who both report upward and manage someone, with cohorts prepared by CTEs.",
  staged_intersection_query,
  10
)
```

## 8) Duplicate-Sensitive Set Operations

`EXCEPT` and `INTERSECT` collapse duplicate rows by default. The `all: true`
variant keeps duplicate multiplicity, which is useful when the repeated value
itself is the thing being compared.

```elixir
department_rows_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([department]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(id)))

manager_department_rows_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([department]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(manager_id)))

duplicate_intersection_query =
  Selecto.intersect(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query, all: true)

duplicate_difference_query =
  Selecto.except(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query, all: true)

SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
  "INTERSECT ALL department rows",
  "Active employee departments that also occur among employees with managers, preserving duplicate rows.",
  duplicate_intersection_query,
  10
)

SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
  "EXCEPT ALL department rows",
  "Active employee department rows left after removing manager-reporting department rows one-for-one.",
  duplicate_difference_query,
  10
)
```

## 9) Overlap Summary Table

This cell turns two cohorts into a compact overlap table before choosing a
set-op query to inspect in detail.

```elixir
cohort_a =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([id]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(id)))

cohort_b =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([id]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(manager_id)))

count_rows = fn query ->
  {:ok, {rows, _columns, _aliases}} = Selecto.execute(query)
  length(rows)
end

overlap_summary = [
  %{slice: "active", rows: count_rows.(cohort_a)},
  %{slice: "has manager", rows: count_rows.(cohort_b)},
  %{slice: "both", rows: count_rows.(Selecto.intersect(cohort_a, cohort_b))},
  %{slice: "active only", rows: count_rows.(Selecto.except(cohort_a, cohort_b))},
  %{slice: "has manager only", rows: count_rows.(Selecto.except(cohort_b, cohort_a))}
]

Kino.DataTable.new(overlap_summary)
```

## 10) Paging Large Set-Op Results

Set operations can be paged after the set-op is composed. Keep a stable
`order_by/2` at the outer query level so page boundaries are repeatable.

```elixir
large_set_query =
  Selecto.union(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query, all: true)
  |> Selecto.order_by({{:raw_sql, "department"}, :asc})

for page <- 0..2 do
  paged_query =
    large_set_query
    |> Selecto.limit(5)
    |> Selecto.offset(page * 5)

  SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
    "Set-op page #{page + 1}",
    "Five-row page from duplicate-preserving department union.",
    paged_query,
    5
  )
end

:ok
```

## 11) SQL Diff: `ALL` vs Duplicate-Collapsing

Inspect the generated SQL for duplicate-collapsing and duplicate-preserving set
operations side by side.

```elixir
set_sql_diff_rows =
  [
    {"UNION", Selecto.union(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query)},
    {"UNION ALL", Selecto.union(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query, all: true)},
    {"INTERSECT", Selecto.intersect(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query)},
    {"INTERSECT ALL", Selecto.intersect(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query, all: true)}
  ]
  |> Enum.map(fn {label, query} ->
    {sql, params} = Selecto.to_sql(query)
    %{operation: label, sql: Selecto.format_sql(sql), params: inspect(params)}
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(set_sql_diff_rows)
```

## 12) CTE-Pre-Normalized Heterogeneous Union

Use CTEs to normalize different source columns into the same output contract
before unioning them.

```elixir
{:ok, normalized_union} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  WITH employee_identity AS (
    SELECT id::text AS entity_id, first_name || ' ' || last_name AS display_name, department AS segment
    FROM employees
    WHERE id IS NOT NULL
  ),
  customer_identity AS (
    SELECT id::text AS entity_id, name AS display_name, country AS segment
    FROM customers
    WHERE id IS NOT NULL
  )
  SELECT 'employee' AS source, entity_id, display_name, segment FROM employee_identity
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'customer' AS source, entity_id, display_name, segment FROM customer_identity
  ORDER BY source, display_name
  LIMIT 20
  """, [])

normalized_union.rows
```

## 13) Challenge: Predict Overlap Counts

Write predictions first, then compare with the actual set-operation counts.

```elixir
predictions = %{
  active: 20,
  has_manager: 20,
  both: 10
}

actuals = %{
  active: count_rows.(cohort_a),
  has_manager: count_rows.(cohort_b),
  both: count_rows.(Selecto.intersect(cohort_a, cohort_b))
}

actuals
|> Enum.map(fn {key, actual} ->
  predicted = Map.fetch!(predictions, key)
  %{cohort: key, predicted: predicted, actual: actual, delta: actual - predicted}
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## 14) Visual Duplicate Difference Indicators

Mark where duplicate-preserving counts diverge from duplicate-collapsing counts.

```elixir
duplicate_indicator_rows =
  [
    {"UNION", Selecto.union(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query), Selecto.union(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query, all: true)},
    {"INTERSECT", Selecto.intersect(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query), Selecto.intersect(department_rows_query, manager_department_rows_query, all: true)}
  ]
  |> Enum.map(fn {operation, collapsed_query, all_query} ->
    collapsed = count_rows.(collapsed_query)
    preserved = count_rows.(all_query)

    %{
      operation: operation,
      collapsed: collapsed,
      duplicate_preserving: preserved,
      differs?: collapsed != preserved,
      delta: preserved - collapsed
    }
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(duplicate_indicator_rows)
```

## 15) Normalized Union with Timestamp and Numeric Coercion

Coerce timestamps and numeric values before unioning heterogeneous sources.

```elixir
{:ok, coerced_union} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  SELECT entity_type, entity_id, event_at, amount
  FROM (
    SELECT 'order' AS entity_type, id::text AS entity_id, inserted_at AS event_at, total::numeric AS amount
    FROM orders
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'customer' AS entity_type, id::text AS entity_id, inserted_at AS event_at, 0::numeric AS amount
    FROM customers
  ) normalized_events
  ORDER BY event_at DESC
  LIMIT 20
  """, [])

coerced_union.rows
```

## 16) Predict EXCEPT Counts After Cohort Changes

Change one cohort definition and compare predicted versus actual `EXCEPT`
counts.

```elixir
high_value_ids =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([id]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(not is_nil(manager_id)))

except_prediction = %{active_except_has_manager: 5}
except_actual = count_rows.(Selecto.except(cohort_a, high_value_ids))

%{prediction: except_prediction.active_except_has_manager, actual: except_actual, delta: except_actual - except_prediction.active_except_has_manager}
```

## 17) Filtered Operands with Outer Ordering and Limit

Current Selecto finalizes a set expression once, after composing all operands.
That keeps bound values from each filtered operand in order and gives the
combined result its own positional `ORDER BY`, `LIMIT`, and `OFFSET` scope.

```elixir
engineering_employees =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(["id", "first_name", "last_name", "title"])
  |> Selecto.filter({"department", "Engineering"})

managers_by_title =
  Selecto.configure(config.employee_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(["id", "first_name", "last_name", "title"])
  |> Selecto.filter({"title", {:like, "%Manager%"}})

composable_set_query =
  Selecto.union(engineering_employees, managers_by_title, all: true)
  |> Selecto.order_by({"title", :asc})
  |> Selecto.limit(12)

{composable_sql, composable_params} = Selecto.to_sql(composable_set_query)
["Engineering", "%Manager%"] = composable_params

%{
  sql: composable_sql,
  params: composable_params,
  result:
    SetOpsLab.Helpers.run(
      "Filtered UNION ALL with outer composition",
      "Each operand keeps its filter; ordering and limit apply to the combined result.",
      composable_set_query,
      12
    )
}
```

